commons shop.messages

uses bynk.locale
uses bynk.locale.types

---
A count `basketSummary` can phrase: 0 to 999. Taking the refined type means a
negative count never reaches a plural template.
---
type ItemCount = Int where InRange(0, 999)

---
The reference locale. Every other block must declare the same codes with the
same placeholders, and `render` falls back to this block for a tag the
bundle doesn't declare.
---
messages "en" @reference {
	"basket.summary" => "{n, plural, one {# item} other {# items}} in your basket"
}

---
French counts 0 as `one`, so an empty basket reads "0 article". The template
only names the arms; the host's plural rules pick which one applies.
---
messages "fr" {
	"basket.summary" => "{n, plural, one {# article} other {# articles}} dans votre panier"
}

---
Polish needs `few` for 2–4 and 22–24 (but not 12–14), and `many` for most
other counts, such as 5 or 12. Each locale declares the arms its own grammar
uses.
---
messages "pl" {
	"basket.summary" => "{n, plural, one {# produkt} few {# produkty} many {# produktów} other {# produktów}} w koszyku"
}

---
The basket summary in `tag`'s language. It lives here, beside the bundle,
because both `bynk.locale` and this bundle export a `render`. A context that
calls this function never has to `uses bynk.locale`, so it never meets the
clash.
---
fn basketSummary(tag: LocaleTag, count: ItemCount) -> String {
	render(tag, withWhole(message("basket.summary"), "n", count))
}
